# WordPress CMS vs Next.js design authority

## Architecture

- **WordPress / Kadence** = content source (text, images, media, links, CMS data).
- **Next.js** = source of truth for visual presentation (typography, color, spacing, layout, interaction).

## Typography hierarchy (required)

Source of truth: `src/lib/kosick-typography-system.ts` (tokens + utility classes).
React wrappers: `src/components/typography`.

Baseline for section title + lead: homepage “Marketing built to move brands forward.”

| Level | Classes / components | Semantic |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Page title | `.page-title` / `<PageTitle>` / `<PageIntro>` | `h1` once per page |
| Section title | `.section-title` / `<SectionTitle>` / `<SectionIntro>` | `h2` |
| Section description | `.section-description` / `<SectionDescription>` | lead under H2 |
| Subsection | `.subsection-title` / `<SubsectionTitle>` | `h3` |
| Card title | `.card-title` / `<CardTitle>` | `h4` when appropriate |
| Eyebrow | `.eyebrow` / `<Eyebrow>` | label above a heading |
| Body | `.body-text` / `<BodyText>` | paragraphs |
| Meta | `.meta-text` / `<MetaText>` | captions / helper |

Rules:

- Do not invent one-off `font-size` values for equivalent content.
- Do not style headings page-by-page; use the shared tokens/classes/components.
- Prefer neutralizing Kadence type over stacking more overrides.
- WordPress HTML may be classified at runtime via `setupTypographyHierarchy()` in `WordPressInteract.tsx`.

## Do

- Extract WordPress content into semantic Next.js components when redesigning UI.
- Style with our own classes (`kosick-*`), CSS modules, or Tailwind — not by fighting Kadence selectors.
- If Kadence CSS conflicts with the design: neutralize or isolate the Kadence rule, then implement the look in Next.js.
- Prefer removing dependency on `kb-*` / `kt-*` / `wp-block-kadence-*` for appearance once a section is redesigned.

## Do not

- Treat Kadence markup or CSS as authoritative for how redesigned UI should look.
- Preserve Kadence styling “because it’s in the imported HTML.”
- Stack more one-off `!important` overrides on Kadence classes when a native Next component would own the UI instead.
- Visually redesign while only doing an architecture cleanup — preserve intended appearance unless the user asks for a redesign.

## Transitional debt

`src/styles/wordpress-compat.css` and client DOM surgery in `WordPressInteract.tsx` are temporary bridges. When touching a section that already has `kosick-*` UI (bento, solutions, why-choose, separators), move ownership toward React components and shrink Kadence-targeted overrides — do not grow them.
